<div class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-5xl flex-col gap-6">
  <%= render FlatPack::PageTitle::Component.new(
    title: "Config",
    subtitle: "Runtime configuration for authorization and root recording resolution.",
    variant: :h1
  ) %>

  <div class="space-y-8 text-sm leading-6 text-(--surface-content-color)">
    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold">Runtime configuration</h2>
      <p class="text-(--surface-muted-content-color)">Configure the engine with a root recording resolver and authorization policy.</p>
      <pre class="overflow-auto rounded-md bg-(--surface-muted-background-color) p-4 text-xs"><code>RecordingStudioCategorisable.configure do |config|
  config.root_recording_resolver = ->(controller) { controller.current_root_recording }
  config.authorization_resolver = ->(controller) { controller.current_user.present? }
end</code></pre>

    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold">categorises</h2>
      <p class="text-(--surface-muted-content-color)">Declare a category-backed attribute and whether it is single-select or multiple-select.</p>
      <pre class="overflow-auto rounded-md bg-(--surface-muted-background-color) p-4 text-xs"><code>class Page < ApplicationRecord
  include RecordingStudioCategorisable::Categorisable

  categorises :status_category_item_recording_id,
    selection: :single,
    category_group_slug: "page-status",
    label: "Status"

  categorises :topic_category_item_recording_ids,
    selection: :multiple,
    category_group_slug: "page-topics",
    label: "Topics"
end</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-2">
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold">Adding a new category field — end-to-end flow</h2>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-xs leading-relaxed text-(--surface-muted-content-color)">
        <p>There are three steps to wire a new category field into a model. The order of the first two steps doesn't matter — you can declare the DSL first and create the group later, or the other way around.</p>

        <div>
          <h3 class="mb-1 text-sm font-medium text-(--surface-content-color)">1. Add the database column</h3>
          <p>Create a migration to add the attribute column on your model's table.</p>
          <pre class="mt-2 overflow-auto rounded-md bg-(--surface-muted-background-color) p-4 text-xs"><code># Single-select (stores one UUID)
add_column :pages, :status_category_item_recording_id, :uuid

# Multi-select (stores an array of UUIDs — PostgreSQL array column)
add_column :pages, :topic_category_item_recording_ids, :uuid, array: true, default: []</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="mb-1 text-sm font-medium text-(--surface-content-color)">2. Declare the field on your model</h3>
          <p>Add a <code>categorises</code> declaration that maps the column to a category group slug.</p>
          <pre class="mt-2 overflow-auto rounded-md bg-(--surface-muted-background-color) p-4 text-xs"><code>class Page < ApplicationRecord
  include RecordingStudioCategorisable::Categorisable

  categorises :status_category_item_recording_id,
    selection: :single,
    category_group_slug: "page-status",
    label: "Status"
end</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="mb-1 text-sm font-medium text-(--surface-content-color)">3. Create the category group and items in the UI</h3>
          <p>Head to <b>Categories</b> in the sidebar and create a group with a slug that matches your <code>category_group_slug</code> (here, <code>page-status</code>). Add items inside it (e.g. Draft, Published, Archived).</p>
          <p>The gem matches the field to the group purely by slug — as long as the slugs match, the category options will appear on your model's form on the next page load.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
